  "content": {
    "id": "p63-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Explain why $x^4+x^2=0$ has only one real solution.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x^2(x^2+1)=0$. Since $x^2+1>0$ for real $x$, the only real solution is $x=0$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x^4+x^2&=0\\\\x^2(x^2+1)&=0\\end{align*}$$ By the null factor law, $x^2=0$ or $x^2+1=0$. The first gives $x=0$. For the second, $$\\begin{align*}x^2+1&=0\\\\x^2&=-1\\end{align*}$$ A real square cannot be negative, so this gives no real solution. Therefore the only real solution is $x=0$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 34
  },
